---
import FAQSection from "@components/article/FAQSection.astro";
import RelatedArticles from "@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

// Article metadata
const title = "How to Expense Home Office Furniture: Complete Guide (2025)";
const description =
  "Learn the rules for deducting home office furniture on your taxes. Desks, chairs, lighting - what qualifies and how to document it properly without IRS trouble.";
const authorSlug = "sarah-mitchell";
const pubDate = new Date("2025-08-10");
const readingTime = 9;
const image = "/images/unsplash/photo-1611269154421-4e27233ac5c7-1200w.webp";
const imageAlt = "Home office furniture setup for expense tracking";

// Table of contents headings
const headings = [
  { text: "Introduction", slug: "introduction", depth: 2 },
  { text: "The Basics Before We Dive In", slug: "basics", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Stuff That Counts (And Stuff That Doesn't)", slug: "what-qualifies", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Writing It Off: Now vs Later", slug: "deduction-methods", depth: 2 },
  { text: "That Depreciation Rabbit Hole", slug: "depreciation", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Keeping the IRS Happy", slug: "documentation", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Employees vs Everyone Else", slug: "employee-vs-self-employed", depth: 2 },
  { text: "The Limits Nobody Hits", slug: "limits", depth: 2 },
  { text: "FAQ", slug: "faq", depth: 2 },
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "My desk gets used for personal stuff too . can I still deduct it?",
    answer:
      "<p>If your desk lives in a <strong>dedicated home office</strong> used exclusively for work, full deduction . even if you occasionally check personal stuff there. In a shared space like the living room? Only deduct the percentage you actually use for work. Eighty percent work use equals eighty percent deductible.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "What about furniture in multiple rooms?",
    answer:
      "<p>Only furniture in your dedicated office counts. That chair in the living room where you sometimes take calls doesn't qualify. The test: is this space used regularly and exclusively for business?</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "How long do I keep receipts?",
    answer:
      "<p>Keep them <strong>seven years minimum</strong>. IRS can audit six years back in some situations. Digital copies work fine . photo the receipt, toss it in cloud storage. Apps like Expensify help too.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Can employees deduct anything?",
    answer:
      "<p><strong>Not at the federal level</strong> through 2025. Some states still allow it . California, New York, and Pennsylvania among them. Self-employed or have 1099 income? You can deduct for that work.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "What about ergonomic stuff specifically?",
    answer:
      '<p>Yep, same as any office furniture. <a href="/gear/best-ergonomic-chairs">Ergonomic chairs</a>, standing desks, monitor arms . all deductible if used for business. Doctor prescribed it for medical reasons? Might also qualify as a medical expense.</p>',
  },
  {
    question: "Can I deduct furniture I already owned?",
    answer:
      "<p>You can depreciate it based on fair market value when you convert it to business use . not what you originally paid. This gets complicated. Buying new and deducting straight is usually simpler.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Section 179 versus depreciation . which is better?",
    answer:
      "<p><strong>Section 179 for most people.</strong> Immediate tax savings, simpler records, and you probably need the deduction more now than years from now. Depreciation only makes sense if you're low income now but expect way higher taxes later.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need a separate room for this?",
    answer:
      "<p>Not necessarily. A dedicated corner with a desk works. A partitioned area. A closet converted to workspace. Just has to be <strong>regular and exclusive business use</strong>. Kitchen table where you eat dinner? Doesn't count.</p>",
  },
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Freelancer Tax Deductions Guide",
    excerpt: "Discover every tax deduction available to freelancers and self-employed workers.",
    href: "/guides/freelancer-tax-deductions-guide",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1554224155-6726b3ff858f-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Tax documents",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Standing Desks for Remote Work",
    excerpt: "Find the perfect standing desk for your home office setup.",
    href: "/gear/best-standing-desks",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1593642702821-c8da6771f0c6-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Standing desk setup",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Ergonomic Chairs",
    excerpt: "Invest in comfort with these top-rated office chairs.",
    href: "/gear/best-ergonomic-chairs",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1580480055273-228ff5388ef8-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Ergonomic office chair",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Finance"
    categorySlug="guides"
    subcategorySlug="finance"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">My First Tax Season Mistake Cost Me $1,200</h2>
  <p>
    Year one of freelancing. I'd upgraded my whole home office. New <a href="/gear/best-standing-desks">standing desk</a>,
    decent <a href="/gear/best-ergonomic-chairs">ergonomic chair</a>, proper lighting. Spent almost $2,000. Tax season
    came around and I had zero receipts. Just... didn't occur to me to save them. My accountant's face told the whole story.
  </p>
  <p>
    I learned the hard way: yes, you can deduct furniture if you're self-employed. But only if you've got the
    documentation to back it up. Nobody at the IRS cares about your nice setup until they do. And then they want
    everything. Here's how to actually claim these deductions without making my mistakes.
  </p>

  <!-- Basics -->
  <h2 id="basics">The Basics Before We Dive In</h2>
  <p>
    Self-employed and your desk sits in a dedicated work spot? Deductible. <a
      href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p946" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">You can write off the
    whole thing immediately through Section 179</a> per the IRS, or spread it across multiple years if that makes
    more sense for your situation.
  </p>
  <p>
    But there are rules:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>You've gotta be self-employed . W-2 folks can't touch this deduction at the federal level anymore</li>
    <li>The furniture has to actually serve a business purpose</li>
    <li>Keep your receipts and maybe snap some photos</li>
    <li>And that home office needs to pass the IRS test for regular and exclusive use</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Let's break down what actually qualifies and where people mess this up.
  </p>

  <!-- What Furniture Qualifies -->
  <h2 id="what-qualifies">Stuff That Counts (And Stuff That Doesn't)</h2>
  <p>
    Pretty much any furniture that helps you work counts. <a href="/gear/best-standing-desks">Standing desks</a>,
    <a href="/gear/best-compact-desks-small-office">compact desks</a>, traditional desks . whatever style you prefer.
    <a href="/gear/best-ergonomic-chairs">Ergonomic chairs</a>, task chairs, kneeling chairs. Filing cabinets,
    bookcases, shelving units. Desk lamps and floor lamps that help you see your work. Even monitor stands and
    keyboard trays if you want to get specific about it.
  </p>

  <h3>What Doesn't Fly</h3>
  <p>
    Decorations and plants unless they're somehow essential to your business (good luck arguing that one).
    That coffee maker in the kitchen. The couch where you sometimes scroll through Slack. General house stuff
    like painting or new floors . though you might prorate the office portion.
  </p>

  <h3>The Quick Test</h3>
  <p>
    Here's my quick test: Would you have bought this thing if you never worked from home?
  </p>
  <p>
    A fifteen hundred dollar ergonomic chair because your back screams after eight hours of work? Business expense.
    A comfy couch where you occasionally reply to emails? That's just a couch.
  </p>

  <!-- Deduction Methods -->
  <h2 id="deduction-methods">Writing It Off: Now vs Later</h2>
  <p>
    Two paths here, and most people overthink this.
  </p>

  <h3>Section 179: Everything Now</h3>
  <p>
    Section 179 lets you deduct everything you paid in the year you bought it. Boom, done.
    <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p946" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">The 2024 limit is over a million
      bucks</a> according to the IRS, so unless you're furnishing a WeWork-sized space, you're fine.
  </p>
  <p>
    Buy an eight hundred dollar standing desk and a five hundred dollar chair this year,
    deduct thirteen hundred on your 2024 taxes.
  </p>

  <h3>Depreciation: Spread It Out</h3>
  <p>
    The alternative is depreciation. Office furniture technically has a seven-year useful life,
    so you'd deduct roughly fourteen percent annually. That same desk spread over seven years
    gets you about a hundred bucks a year in deductions.
  </p>

  <h3>Which to Pick?</h3>
  <p>
    Most freelancers should grab Section 179. Money now beats money later . that's just how
    time value works. Plus less tracking over the years.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only go depreciation if you're having a low income year and expect to earn way more down the road.
  </p>

  <!-- Depreciation Explained -->
  <h2 id="depreciation">That Depreciation Rabbit Hole</h2>
  <p>
    If you do go the depreciation route, the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p946" target="_blank"
                                                rel="nofollow">IRS uses something called MACRS</a>. Modified Accelerated
    Cost Recovery System . catchy name, right?
  </p>
  <p>
    The percentages aren't even splits. Year one gives you about fourteen percent, year two jumps
    to almost twenty-five percent, then it tapers down. By year seven and eight you're getting
    single digits. The math works out to a full write-off over time.
  </p>

  <h3>Bonus Depreciation</h3>
  <p>
    There's also bonus depreciation . sixty percent for 2024, forty percent for 2025, twenty percent
    for 2026, then it phases out completely. But honestly, Section 179 does basically the same thing
    with less headache for furniture purchases.
  </p>

  <!-- Documentation Requirements -->
  <h2 id="documentation">Keeping the IRS Happy</h2>
  <p>
    Documentation sounds boring because it is. But the alternative is losing your deductions
    during an audit, and that's worse boring.
  </p>

  <h3>What to Keep for Every Purchase</h3>
  <p>
    For every piece of furniture: save the receipt showing what you bought and when, keep proof
    you actually paid (credit card statement works), take a photo of the thing sitting in your
    office, and jot a quick note about why you needed it.
  </p>

  <h3>Your Home Office Itself</h3>
  <p>
    For the office itself: measure the square footage and know your home's total. Take pictures
    showing your dedicated workspace. A quick floor plan sketch showing where the office sits
    doesn't hurt.
  </p>

  <h3>How Long to Keep Everything</h3>
  <p>
    How long to keep this stuff? <a
      href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"
      target="_blank" rel="nofollow">At least three years from when you filed</a> says the IRS, but seven years is
    safer.
    For anything you're depreciating, keep records until the depreciation finishes plus three more years.
  </p>
  <p>
    Just dump it all in a Google Drive folder organized by tax year and forget about it.
  </p>

  <!-- Employee vs Self-Employed -->
  <h2 id="employee-vs-self-employed">Employees vs Everyone Else</h2>
  <p>
    Here's where things get frustrating for a lot of people.
  </p>

  <h3>Self-Employed Folks</h3>
  <p>
    Self-employed folks . freelancers, 1099 contractors, business owners . get the full deduction.
    File it on Schedule C or your business return. Just need that regular and exclusive use situation.
  </p>

  <h3>W-2 Employees</h3>
  <p>
    W-2 employees? Basically nothing at the federal level. <a
      href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/tax-reform-eliminated-deduction-for-employee-business-expenses" target="_blank"
      rel="nofollow">The 2017 tax overhaul killed that deduction
    through 2025</a> per the IRS. Some states still allow it . California, New York, and Pennsylvania among them . so
    check your local rules. Or ask your employer to reimburse you, which is tax-free on your end.
  </p>

  <h3>Got Both?</h3>
  <p>
    Got both? Like you're employed full-time but also freelance on the side? You can only deduct
    the freelance portion. Twenty hours freelancing plus forty hours at your day job means you can
    deduct a third of that furniture cost against your freelance income.
  </p>

  <!-- Limits and Caps -->
  <h2 id="limits">The Limits Nobody Hits</h2>
  <p>
    Section 179 caps at over a million bucks for 2024. Unless you're outfitting an actual office
    building, you're nowhere near that.
  </p>

  <h3>Home Office Method Limits</h3>
  <p>
    The simplified home office method . five bucks per square foot, maxing at three hundred square
    feet . caps at fifteen hundred total. But furniture gets deducted separately, not counted in that cap.
  </p>
  <p>
    Regular method? No ceiling on furniture specifically, though your total home office deduction
    can't exceed what you made from the business. Any excess rolls forward to next year.
  </p>

  <h3>The Practical Reality</h3>
  <p>
    Practical reality: unreasonably expensive stuff might invite questions. A ten thousand dollar
    desk when you're pulling fifty grand a year might raise eyebrows. Just document why you needed
    it if you're going high-end.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Get Your Deduction Already</h2>
  <p>
    Your home office furniture is a legitimate write-off that puts actual money back in your pocket.
    Not complicated if you just stay organized.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quick checklist: photograph your setup, measure the space, save your receipts digitally, note
    why you bought each thing, use Section 179 for the immediate win, and get a tax pro involved
    if your situation has layers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Build yourself a comfortable workspace . and let the tax savings make it easier to justify.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
